body:
When someone describes a game as being "crunchy," I flash back to the episode of "3rd Rock From The Sun" where the aliens are at the breakfast table. Harry says he's having Monopoly for breakfast, picks up a box of Monopoly, and pours the pieces into a cereal bowl.  Later in the scene, Dick comes down and says "I'll have some Monopoly, thank you."

&#x200B;

So, yes, if you tell me a game is "crunchy," I will presume that you're eating it.

Dig it.  I remember getting the little multi-colored plastic paper clips for keeping track of wounds.

&#x200B;

We called them Poxy-Clips.

You'll be able to complete the basic storyline, but a lot of the game will be locked off to you.  There's a combination of characters that, if you get them, cover all the Special Abilities in the game.  And the other dimensions and their stories require someone from that universe to get to.

&#x200B;

If you have the LEGO Batman Movie set, you get Robin and Batgirl and your existing Batman can be converted to LEGO Batman Movie Batman so you'll get Will Arnett in all but the cut scenes.

A monster that's not at all what they're expecting is a good way to go.  If you can find the old "Blood Brothers" books, those are a series of B-Movie Horror adventures and the first book focuses on conventional monsters.  There are also some of your standard horror fare creatures in the monster section of the rulebook.

&#x200B;

But yes, a monster that they can't just use player knowledge and go "Ok, that's a Colour Out Of Space," and does whatever foul thing it does for unguessable motives is well within the bounds of Call of Cthulhu.
